WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:12.640
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: May we then move on to item 6 on the agenda, which is around creating

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:18.240
A genomic medicine service.  If I can invite Professor Sue Hill who is Chief Scientific Officer to join us

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:25.000
but I'm going to invite Bruce to introduce the paper and then Sue to go into more depth

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.000
Bruce, please may we start with you

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.100
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: This will be the third time,
I think, that we have brought a discussion

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:37.989
of genomics to the Board, and one of our roles,
I think, as a Board is to deal with the problems

00:00:37.989 --> 00:00:43.870
of today with an eye to the future. It seems
to me that there are three technologies that

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:48.309
are going to have a really big impact on the
practice of medicine, or healthcare in its

00:00:48.309 --> 00:00:53.320
broadest sense, over the course of the next
decade: mobile technology, artificial intelligence

00:00:53.320 --> 00:01:01.079
and genomics. X-rays enabled us to see inside
the body, the microscope enabled us to see

00:01:01.079 --> 00:01:07.200
inside cells; genomics enables us to understand
how cells work, and to understand the basis

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:14.390
of many inherited and non-communicable diseases.
One thing that is absolutely clear is that

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:19.830
we lead the world in this sort of science.
Indeed, life sciences are a key plank of the

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:25.479
emerging industrial strategy for the government,
so it makes sense that we should bring this

00:01:25.479 --> 00:01:32.570
expertise to our patients through application,
where possible, in the National Health Service.

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:37.920
If we look at genomics and the sequencing
of the human genome, it originally took ten

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:46.230
years and cost about $3 billion; now it is
down to under 24 hours and about £300. The

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:52.790
fall in cost starts to make this a realistic
possibility after a number of false dawns,

00:01:52.790 --> 00:02:00.850
so Sue is going to outline at a very high
level our emerging thoughts and strategy as

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:09.950
to how we might start to bring the benefits
of genomics into the National Health Service.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:17.370
&gt;&gt;Sue Hill: The Board paper sets out an ambitious
future vision for the NHS to fully embed genomics

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:24.200
and other next-generation diagnostics into
routine care and, as Bruce has already outlined,

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:28.650
aligned with the government aspirations to
build research and industry collaborations

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:34.790
with the NHS in the life sciences sector.
The proposed genomic medicine service and

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:41.069
operating model outlined in the Board paper
builds upon both the long history in the UK

00:02:41.069 --> 00:02:47.400
of scientific advances in the field of genetics
and genomics, and on the investment in NHS

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:55.310
genetic testing services for now over 50 years.
The NHS occupies a unique position as an integrated

00:02:55.310 --> 00:03:01.530
health system with access to longitudinal
records; both important to the uptake of genomic

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:08.620
technologies. Now is the time to capitalise
on this unique position to take a step change

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:14.519
in the uptake of genomic technologies, coupled
with other technological advances; for example,

00:03:14.519 --> 00:03:18.379
in digital imaging and in data analytics.

00:03:18.379 --> 00:03:25.629
Although the NHS can currently test for over
4,800 genes in a variety of different ways,

00:03:25.629 --> 00:03:31.840
and often in a set of series of tests for
patients, the use of whole-genome sequencing

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:38.209
enables all the letters in an individual's
genetic code to be determined in one test,

00:03:38.209 --> 00:03:44.340
enabling potentially important genetic alterations
to be identified above and beyond those that

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:50.569
we can test for now. The adoption of this
technology into the NHS would enable some

00:03:50.569 --> 00:03:57.099
of the current testing undertaken in the NHS
to be replaced, and a greater diagnostic yield

00:03:57.099 --> 00:04:04.440
to be delivered with greater efficiency and
lower cost. In terms of that greater diagnostic

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:13.530
yield, that can be between 25-50% enhancements
to diagnosis, depending on the condition.

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:19.830
The major catalyst for the approach that we
outline has come from the world-leading 100,000

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:25.310
Genomes project, which is sequencing whole
genomes in participants with rare disease

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:32.620
and inherited disease, and cancer. The transformational
change required to deliver an overall genomic

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:36.610
medicine service that we set out in the Board
paper is already well underway, through the

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:43.930
work of the 13 NHS Genomic Medicine Centres
established by NHS England to deliver the

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:50.860
90,000-sample commitment to the 100,000 Genomes
project. Through these established partnerships

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:57.580
and infrastructure they have created across
populations of between three million to five

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:03.139
million, the Genomic Medicine Centres are
providing opportunities for other significant

00:05:03.139 --> 00:05:08.650
service changes. Whilst some challenges with
the delivery of the 100,000 Genomes project

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:15.360
remain, particularly around cancer, significant
progress is being made, and the NHS has established

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:22.750
world-leading pathways and protocols to get
the most out of whole-genome sequencing for

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:27.940
participants. Cancer alliances and vanguards
are important in supporting these and other

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:33.460
initiatives, to improve recruitment but also
to enable genomic pathways of cancer care

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:35.330
for the future.

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:39.780
The use of whole-genome sequencing in the
project is already delivering benefits to

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:45.390
the NHS in informing the way it will be utilised
in the future, as part of a comprehensive

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:51.740
genomic testing approach. For patients and
their families, the project is already providing

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:58.570
enhanced diagnoses, delivering improvement
in outcomes for more targeted treatments,

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:03.730
and enabling patients to enter clinical trials
that they would not have otherwise been able

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:12.889
to do. It will also help to optimise medicines
for the future and reduce adverse drug reactions

00:06:12.889 --> 00:06:17.850
that are a significant cause, for example,
of hospital admissions.

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:24.789
As we move forward with establishing a genomic
medicine service through to 2021, the continued

00:06:24.789 --> 00:06:30.211
partnership with Genomics England remains
a critical part of the operating model. This

00:06:30.211 --> 00:06:35.990
will build upon the considerable expertise
they have developed in the area of whole-genome

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:43.550
sequencing and analytical platforms, and how
they can bring about a semi-automated interpretation

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:50.080
pipeline, but also in continuing to develop
the UK Genomic Knowledge Database, which is

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:57.440
important if we are going to continually deliver
enhanced diagnoses for patients. To take this

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:03.639
forward now, we need to begin the re-procurement
of the existing genetic laboratories in the

00:07:03.639 --> 00:07:11.550
NHS into a consolidated genomic laboratory
hub network, that is co ordinated and underpinned

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:17.540
by a comprehensive genomic testing strategy
for rare and inherited disease, and cancer,

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:25.780
and through the alignment with the commissioning
system from CCGs and, importantly, with specialised

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:30.150
commissioning, but also being embedded in
STP plans.

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:35.490
This is on the basis of securing whole-genome
sequencing provision to become operational

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:42.460
in the second half of 2018/2019, and delivered
as part of the genomic testing repertoire

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:48.320
that will be available in the NHS. If the
Board is content to approve the high-level

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:54.660
approach set out in this paper, we will develop
our detailed implementation plans and the

00:07:54.660 --> 00:08:01.700
business case to make this ambition vision
a reality.

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:11.550
&gt;&gt;John Burn: It is a great privilege and pleasure
to be a member of your team, so I actually

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:15.580
know more about the detail of this, but I
would like to just as a Board member raise

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:21.470
the issue of cost and benefit at the high
level. Clearly, I am equipoised between the

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:26.379
benefits as an academic geneticist and keen
to see this happen, and yet the costs as a

00:08:26.379 --> 00:08:34.000
member of the workforce, somebody has to pay
for it. The two questions that are in my mind

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:41.399
are: as we move towards a more integrated
genomic medicines service, are you comfortable

00:08:41.399 --> 00:08:47.029
that we can control activity? Because clearly,
my experience in my lifetime is, once people

00:08:47.029 --> 00:08:51.630
get an idea for a new test, everyone wants
to order it. So, as we spread this out, how

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:58.230
do we manage that activity so that we actually
do the tests effectively? Which flips over

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:03.610
onto the workforce in terms of the impact
on the workforce being expected to deliver

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:09.100
that, and perhaps also the existing budget
being expected to carry it rather than it

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:16.410
being seen as a new development. How do you
feel we are going to cope with that?

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:26.019
&gt;&gt;Sue Hill: In terms of activity, John, you
are absolutely right. What underpins the introduction

00:09:26.019 --> 00:09:35.269
of a comprehensive genomic testing strategy
is a clear and coherent order communications

00:09:35.269 --> 00:09:41.690
system, which means the way in which tests
can be ordered will be set out for different

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:47.010
conditions, and the type of test that will
be applied for different conditions will be

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:53.980
based on evidence, and evidence for the utility,
either in terms of the utility that we can

00:09:53.980 --> 00:10:01.781
get from whole-genome sequencing against the
cost, or for doing a single gene test, because

00:10:01.781 --> 00:10:06.940
it is relatively cheap to do and provides
all the information that is necessary for

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:13.820
that condition. A critical part of going forward,
and outlined in this paper, is the need for

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:23.260
there to be co ordination of the laboratory
hubs infrastructure that will be created,

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:30.950
such that the activity is clearly monitored
and managed, and the advice that is provided

00:10:30.950 --> 00:10:38.410
to the commissioning system really drives
what those hubs are able to order and provide

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:41.140
for their populations in the future.

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:47.380
The second point you make is around the impact
on the workforce. What we know with a whole-genome

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:54.320
sequence provider being outside of the NHS
delivery chain, is that this will require

00:10:54.320 --> 00:11:04.500
a new cadre of expertise in the NHS to interpret
whole-genome sequence data, so part of the

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:11.209
strategy will be to develop the workforce
to undertake and have that type of expertise,

00:11:11.209 --> 00:11:16.240
as well as thinking about what will remain
in the NHS to be delivered and how efficiently

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:20.080
that can be delivered by an appropriate matching
of skill mix.

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:31.280
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Sue, as well as being very
excited by the potential of this technology,

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:36.640
which we are, as a Board, we obviously have
to be mindful of the scale investment required

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:42.430
and the financial consequences of the decisions
that we make. What I would like to ask is

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:47.920
that, in the business case you bring forward,
could we please look at, under the umbrella

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:53.150
of the overall strategic approach, what the
options are and what the financial consequences

00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:55.020
would be of those choices?

00:11:55.020 --> 00:12:01.490
&gt;&gt;Sue Hill: Yes, I think that is a very good
suggestion, and I would like to reassure Board

00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:07.040
members: the economic modelling that will
underpin the business case is being done in

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:14.330
conjunction with health economists at Oxford
University, and very much informed by evidence

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:16.230
that we are gathering from across the world.

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:17.290
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Thank you.

00:12:17.290 --> 00:12:27.519
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: The value of the genomic programme
in terms of paving the way for personalised

00:12:27.519 --> 00:12:34.050
medicine is very clear, but we also in a sense
have a national asset that we are developing

00:12:34.050 --> 00:12:40.820
here in the context of the bigger life sciences
industrial strategy, because it has the potential

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:47.519
to attract pharma, attract research, speed
up trials, help us with the med-tech sector,

00:12:47.519 --> 00:12:55.851
as well. Could you just say a little bit about
how, as you evolve the genomics asset, you

00:12:55.851 --> 00:13:03.399
are thinking about its role in terms of supporting
a much more vibrant life sciences business

00:13:03.399 --> 00:13:05.389
economy in the UK?

00:13:05.389 --> 00:13:12.321
&gt;&gt;Sue Hill: If I may use the example of what
is happening currently in the 100,000 Genomes

00:13:12.321 --> 00:13:17.370
project, and ongoing dialogue with the NHS,
that may illustrate, but I think there is

00:13:17.370 --> 00:13:26.480
a broader question that I would defer to Simon
to answer about the more strategic positioning

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:35.930
of the NHS with the life sciences sector,
and the way that that will be taken forward.

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:45.139
Within the 100,000 Genomes project, through
obtaining participant consent for the use

00:13:45.139 --> 00:13:51.839
of their de-identified data to enter into
what is becoming a UK genomic knowledge database,

00:13:51.839 --> 00:13:57.780
we are starting to understand how that can
be interrogated by researchers across the

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:07.550
world, so to start to align the research questions
with the research funders; in an international

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:08.690
effort, often.

00:14:08.690 --> 00:14:15.540
&gt;&gt;Secondly, how, through a pre-competitive
consortium of industry players, the value

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:23.110
of that data and how that data can be enhanced,
really to start to think about how the NHS

00:14:23.110 --> 00:14:30.199
and real-world data could be utilised to drive
clinical trials in a different way. I think

00:14:30.199 --> 00:14:37.160
part of the work going forward in genomics
is how we as NHS England can enter into a

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:45.399
new dialogue with industry who are interested
in doing clinical trials in this area, and

00:14:45.399 --> 00:14:49.459
what the NHS can benefit from that collaboration.

00:14:49.459 --> 00:14:54.829
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I think it is important to
note also that with GeL, we have had a bridge

00:14:54.829 --> 00:14:59.040
to industry. It has been quite important to
have a company that is separate from us and

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:04.350
separate from the Department of Health, but
which can also release data that is of value

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:10.320
to industry on an anonymised basis. Part of
the business case for the future is going

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:15.839
to need to consider how we maintain these
relationships and how we maintain the other

00:15:15.839 --> 00:15:21.100
benefits that GeL has brought to the system
as a whole; particularly around data curation,

00:15:21.100 --> 00:15:26.910
which is of great importance to us. I think
Noel, with future relationships with NHS Digital,

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:31.800
is taking on many of those responsibilities.
There is actually quite a complex transitional

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:37.019
process as we go through what is currently
pretty much the proof of concept, which is

00:15:37.019 --> 00:15:41.649
the 100,000 Genomes. We know we have that,
I think, now reasonably well established with

00:15:41.649 --> 00:15:46.070
rare diseases. It is a bit more difficult
still with cancer, for a number of reasons

00:15:46.070 --> 00:15:53.110
that Sue outlines, but then into I suppose
initial phases of transfer into the clinic

00:15:53.110 --> 00:16:00.029
more generally, before the full roll-out,
and that transitional process will also include

00:16:00.029 --> 00:16:04.880
further work on cancer so that we can bring
it up to a level where it will be capable

00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:08.380
of bearing the clinical weight that it will
ultimately have to do.

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:17.690
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Sue, just an observation
that this all sounds hugely interesting and

00:16:17.690 --> 00:16:25.480
exciting and, as Bruce said, is a next wave
of clinical activity which opens up things

00:16:25.480 --> 00:16:30.920
we have never even dreamt of. It also has
a slightly scary connotation to it, or it

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:39.800
could have, and quite a lot of misinformation
about what this could say or could not do

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:48.220
relative to insurance, relative to all sorts
of aspects. Therefore, in the next discussion

00:16:48.220 --> 00:16:53.490
around the business case, I would encourage
us to be thinking about what investment we

00:16:53.490 --> 00:17:04.550
need to be making in education and engagement
with citizens of the country, with our clinicians,

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:08.449
to make sure that everyone understands what
this really is and what its opportunities

00:17:08.449 --> 00:17:15.880
are, as opposed to what could be construed
as being somewhat threatening. I think we

00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:20.839
need to think of the human dynamic, not just
the infrastructure, the science and the data;

00:17:20.839 --> 00:17:23.780
there is another big element to this that
is very important.

00:17:23.780 --> 00:17:27.410
&gt;&gt;Sue Hill: Thank you, David. I think that
is a very good point, and you will note in

00:17:27.410 --> 00:17:33.919
the Board paper, we did outline some of the
public and patient engagement work that has

00:17:33.919 --> 00:17:39.590
happened to date. That has been an integral
part of each of our 13 Genomic Medicine Centres

00:17:39.590 --> 00:17:46.800
from the very start, and they have very active
groups that are participating. This is the

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:54.320
start of a conversation with the public, especially
as we use genomics to really drive our personalised

00:17:54.320 --> 00:18:02.600
medicine approaches, and in some instances,
on the basis of any genomic mutation that

00:18:02.600 --> 00:18:08.230
is identified, a certain set of treatments
may be inappropriate for patients. It is a

00:18:08.230 --> 00:18:12.110
different conversation, and one that we need
to build in.

00:18:12.110 --> 00:18:18.790
Secondly, the clinical change model that you
allude to, from primary care right up to secondary

00:18:18.790 --> 00:18:25.740
and tertiary care, is an important dynamic
here, and part of the work going forward is

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:32.270
to work closely with the Academy of Medical
Royal Colleges, but also with all the professional

00:18:32.270 --> 00:18:38.320
groups; with the Royal College of Nursing
and Midwifery, as well as the whole multi-professional

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:40.300
team that will be critical in delivering this.

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:45.780
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: The primary interfaces always
will be providing the services of our GPs

00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:51.309
and our nurses and practice supports, and
how we equip them to have good conversations

00:18:51.309 --> 00:18:53.320
and support that is critical, I think.

00:18:53.320 --> 00:18:58.920
&gt;&gt;Sue Hill: David, if I may just come back
to you, earlier this week we had a conversation

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:04.401
with a set of interested professionals around
familial hypercholesterolemia testing, which

00:19:04.401 --> 00:19:11.960
can be in terms of understanding and determining
individuals' genotype. That is really important

00:19:11.960 --> 00:19:19.520
in either starting treatment earlier or starting
new types of treatment. What was interesting

00:19:19.520 --> 00:19:24.710
was, a lot of the conversation was on how
we could ensure that primary care had access

00:19:24.710 --> 00:19:30.570
to those types of test that will be built
into our comprehensive genomic testing strategy

00:19:30.570 --> 00:19:33.100
for the future.

00:19:33.100 --> 00:19:41.590
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: In relation to that, I
am just curious about how this relates to

00:19:41.590 --> 00:19:47.150
inequalities and the inverse care law, and
how this whole programme helps to close the

00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:48.150
gap.

00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:54.150
&gt;&gt;Sue Hill: Thank you, Victor, for asking
that question, because some of the current

00:19:54.150 --> 00:19:59.980
genetic testing in the NHS at the moment does
not reach all the parts of the population

00:19:59.980 --> 00:20:10.590
it needs to, and because there is no overarching
co ordination of the provision, then we have

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:16.280
had no way in the past of either aligning
the commissioning system with that, or really

00:20:16.280 --> 00:20:22.870
ensuring that locally, there is a focus in
those clinical specialities where patients

00:20:22.870 --> 00:20:30.809
would benefit. I think this is a clear part
of the overall strategy that we outline here,

00:20:30.809 --> 00:20:35.010
to start to address the inequalities.

00:20:35.010 --> 00:20:40.270
What has been interesting as part of the NHS
contribution to the 100,000 Genomes project

00:20:40.270 --> 00:20:48.340
is that we are starting to see previously
unknown rare disease presenting into the project,

00:20:48.340 --> 00:20:54.591
rather than what has been known but there
has just been no diagnosis. I think it is

00:20:54.591 --> 00:21:01.080
starting to happen, but we have to help to
ensure that this is part of our systematic

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:02.799
embedding in the future.

00:21:02.799 --> 00:21:07.190
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Are we measuring that?
Are we measuring its impact in that regard?

00:21:07.190 --> 00:21:10.921
&gt;&gt;Sue Hill: We are starting to measure that.
It is quite difficult to measure, because

00:21:10.921 --> 00:21:18.500
it has actually never been looked for in any
detail before, but we are certainly measuring

00:21:18.500 --> 00:21:24.909
the equitable access to the 100,000 Genomes
project and using that to drive our strategic

00:21:24.909 --> 00:21:28.140
approach to reducing inequalities in the future.

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:35.970
&gt;&gt;Wendy Becker: I have been incredibly impressed
with some of the early results coming out

00:21:35.970 --> 00:21:39.050
of Birmingham, to see some of these things
already feeding into our medical processes,

00:21:39.050 --> 00:21:44.542
and that is incredibly exciting. As a typical
patient/consumer, when would I expect to have

00:21:44.542 --> 00:21:50.330
a conversation with my GP about this, or in
the medical system? For an everyday person,

00:21:50.330 --> 00:21:53.280
when is this a reasonable dream?

00:21:53.280 --> 00:22:06.210
&gt;&gt;Sue Hill: At the moment, obviously, the
access to either the project or to existing

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:17.220
genetic testing is very focused and polarised.
The more systematic access will start over

00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:23.520
the next three years, but will take us probably
a ten-year journey to get there in totality.

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:30.059
What is important with this is to start to
engage early with general practice, and it

00:22:30.059 --> 00:22:34.020
just so happens that later today I have a
meeting with the Royal College of General

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:40.580
Practice to begin that conversation, or to
take forward further conversations with them,

00:22:40.580 --> 00:22:47.570
about engaging family practitioners. What
has happened, I think, over the last two to

00:22:47.570 --> 00:22:52.580
three years since we have been working on
this project is, there is a greater awareness

00:22:52.580 --> 00:22:59.559
within the NHS of this, but there is more
that we need to do. It will be between that

00:22:59.559 --> 00:23:02.250
three- to ten-year journey before this will
be available.

00:23:02.250 --> 00:23:08.240
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: I think we are in the foothills
of it, Wendy. At the moment if, in your family,

00:23:08.240 --> 00:23:14.809
there are people with certain types of genetic
anomalies, you will get access to it. If you

00:23:14.809 --> 00:23:21.870
have certain types of cancer, you may get
access to it, but that is just the beginning,

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:27.650
because we do not even know necessarily exactly
where this is going to take us, but we are

00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:31.929
clear that it is going to take us into a much
deeper understanding of disease and provide

00:23:31.929 --> 00:23:34.570
opportunities for new treatments.

00:23:34.570 --> 00:23:41.531
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Sue,
for the paper and for the presentation. I

00:23:41.531 --> 00:23:46.281
think you can take it from what you have heard
around the boardroom table: A) that the Board

00:23:46.281 --> 00:23:52.539
notes what has been proceeding, what has been
developing under the 100,000 Genomes programme;

00:23:52.539 --> 00:24:00.520
B) I think is warmly endorsing the high-level
strategy; but C) with still a recognition

00:24:00.520 --> 00:24:06.390
that there are many as-yet unresolved issues,
and many variables to be addressed in bringing

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:11.970
this programme more firmly into the clinic.
Over the next two years, which are, as I was

00:24:11.970 --> 00:24:15.600
saying before, very much a transition period,
there are a lot of issues that we are going

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:25.040
to have to sort out. With the volume and with
the funding, and with the distribution, I

00:24:25.040 --> 00:24:30.900
think to Victor's point, the setting-up of
13 GMCs across the country has been a fundamental

00:24:30.900 --> 00:24:36.049
element of securing equity of access to the
technology across the country, and that is

00:24:36.049 --> 00:24:39.350
something that we need to continue to embed.

00:24:39.350 --> 00:24:43.610
I think the final point though, is that this
is an area in which we are currently the world

00:24:43.610 --> 00:24:48.820
leader, and I would be very keen to ensure
that we did everything possible to maintain

00:24:48.820 --> 00:24:53.720
that leadership, and to be the country from
which other people bought the ideas and the

00:24:53.720 --> 00:24:59.409
technology rather than the country that ends
up buying it from somewhere else, having been

00:24:59.409 --> 00:25:05.830
falling behind the path that we need to go
on. We will be coming back to this over the

00:25:05.830 --> 00:25:11.169
next year or two, because it is of such fundamental
importance to the future of diagnostics and

00:25:11.169 --> 00:25:16.600
therapeutics in the NHS. Thank you very much,
Sue, for bringing us up to date, and we look

00:25:16.600 --> 00:25:21.140
forward to following further developments
with enthusiasm.

